WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.287
- Okay, so this is the TCL 40 NXTPAPER,

00:00:03.287 --> 00:00:05.130
and at first glance, it might not appear

00:00:05.130 --> 00:00:06.660
to be a particularly special phone.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.390
Like, this is a $200 budget Android phone.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.640
It's got a 5000 milliamp power battery,

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.670
a pretty standard issue
50 megapixel main camera

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.350
flanked by a 5 megapixel ultrawide

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:17.670
and a two megapixel macro,

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:21.540
and inside, a Mediatek G88
processor, 8 gigs of RAM,

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:23.130
and 256 gigs of storage.

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:24.720
This design is pretty basic.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.610
It's squared up, nothing too offensive,

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.890
a solid blue color with
some sparkles in it,

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:31.770
but it has exactly one
really interesting feature

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:33.570
that's caught my attention,

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:34.530
and that's the screen.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.220
So this screen, you're
looking at a 6.78 inch,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:41.040
flat, 1080p, 90 hertz display,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.530
but when you put it up
next to another phone,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:44.850
that's when you immediately notice

00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:46.590
something is very different about it,

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:50.970
mainly that there are no
reflections at all, none,

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:51.990
well, almost none,

00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.430
so this phone is called the NXTPAPER

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.080
because it has what TCL is calling

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.610
a full-color electronic paper display

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:04.440
to deliver a reflection-free, full-color,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.480
paper-like visual experience,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.370
which looks way better,

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:10.590
has much less fatigue on the eyes,

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:13.110
and also feels better in the hand.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:14.460
And so my first thought,
when I first saw this,

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.580
was, okay, why doesn't
every phone have this?

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.790
Like, how is it a super cheap budget phone

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:23.190
that's the first one I've
seen to pull this off,

00:01:23.190 --> 00:01:25.710
and why aren't more phones doing this?

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:27.270
What am I missing here?

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:30.307
And so of course, I had to use
it to get all these answers.

00:01:30.307 --> 00:01:33.240
So, okay, TCL calls it the NXTPAPER

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.440
because they'd like you to think of it

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:36.660
as just like an e-paper display.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.310
If you've ever seen a Kindle

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.950
or any of the other various
E Ink displays out there,

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.800
that tech is actually
kinda sick, it's amazing.

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:45.930
It minimizes fatigue on the eyes,

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.840
and it looks and feels like
something's literally printed

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:51.510
on a piece of paper, like an
newspaper, in front of you.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.310
It works great in all
sorts of different lighting

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.460
because it's literally
rearranging actual ink

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:57.570
underneath the display.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.690
But this is actually not that.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.230
Instead, it's an LCD display
with a textured glass on top

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:08.670
and some software tricks to
resemble a paper-like display

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:11.790
and deliver almost all of
the eye comfort benefits

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:12.623
that come with it,

00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:15.000
so the result is basically

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:16.620
what looks like a normal smartphone screen

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.730
with zero reflections,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.100
and I am very into that.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.210
Like, look at this screen

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.840
next to a glossy regular
smartphone glass screen.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.250
Typically, when I review phones,

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:27.870
like, I have to spend a lot of effort

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:30.330
shielding these displays from reflections.

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.980
That's a little known fact in this,

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.330
I guess it's kinda inside baseball,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:35.700
but when you shoot reflective
things, it is a challenge.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.350
I have to angle the phone certain ways,

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.240
I have to shoot in certain environments,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.940
and I often use what we call a flag here,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.640
which is literally just to
slide it in at a certain angle

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.240
to block reflections and make
the screen easier to see.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.073
It's a whole thing.

00:02:49.073 --> 00:02:50.340
But with this matte display,

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:53.070
I mean, I just don't need any
of that special treatment,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:55.200
like, it's happy to show up at any angle,

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:57.810
and the angles that would
normally have a giant reflection,

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.880
it just kinda looks a little bit lighter,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.790
a little faded, but
still actually readable,

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.870
and then when you're straight on,

00:03:03.870 --> 00:03:05.700
or just kinda regularly reading,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.430
it's nice and soft, and it's
less stressful on the eyes.

00:03:08.430 --> 00:03:11.700
It is kinda weirdly a
little bit paper-like,

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.530
like some of those paper-like displays.

00:03:13.530 --> 00:03:16.770
So almost all of this
is enabled by hardware,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:18.750
so there are a bunch of extra layers

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.560
between the actual LCD display and you,

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.300
including this textured glass,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.380
and so that's all what gives
it this anti-reflective look.

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:29.490
There's also some other benefits,

00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:32.370
like physically limiting
the amount of blue light

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.203
that makes it through,

00:03:33.203 --> 00:03:34.800
so instead of a special
mode in the software,

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.930
there is just physically less blue light

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:38.910
making it through an additional filter

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:39.743
into your eyes.

00:03:39.743 --> 00:03:41.730
And another underrated one, the feel.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.310
I mean, literally, the way the glass feels

00:03:44.310 --> 00:03:45.270
under your finger.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:47.880
It kinda has this soft touch effect

00:03:47.880 --> 00:03:49.170
thanks to the slight texture.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.610
So you can actually see the difference

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.780
up at the cutout at the top.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.520
You obviously can't do textured glass

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:54.780
over the front-facing camera,

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:56.670
because that would ruin the photos,

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.680
not that they're amazing
photos in the first place,

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:00.210
but that's the most obvious spot

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:01.440
where you can see that difference

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.650
between the matte finish and glossy glass.

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.230
And my finger just runs over this glass

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.517
almost the same way that it
does on the back of the phone.

00:04:10.517 --> 00:04:13.492
(rubbing with no sound)

00:04:13.492 --> 00:04:16.170
(rubbing creates swishing)

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:20.010
So I think all this is
shaping up to be pretty cool,

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:23.820
the matte finish, the
basically zero reflections,

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.540
the much lower eye strain,
the soft touch finish.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.800
There's also stylus support,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:30.210
as TCL makes a stylus

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:31.830
that actually works with
this type of screen.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:33.570
So what's the catch?

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:37.263
Why don't more phones have this on them?

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:40.500
And you're probably one
step ahead of me already,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:42.900
which is, yeah, there are some downsides.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:43.733
So first of all,

00:04:43.733 --> 00:04:45.840
a bunch of extra layers
on the display itself

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.160
is gonna have some obvious drawbacks,

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.800
but mainly, it's just that
they're cutting a lot of light,

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:51.930
like, it hurts a lot of sharpness

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:53.400
and just total light
getting through to you.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:56.580
So this is the absolute
maximum 100% brightness

00:04:56.580 --> 00:04:57.420
of this screen,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.980
and it's 450 nits,

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.920
so it's good enough to read
indoors, as you've seen,

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.650
but outdoors, it's gonna get
pretty quickly washed out

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.150
and is disappointingly dim.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.980
Now, again, this is a $200 phone,

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:10.620
so I think if this was
an even higher-end phone

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:13.080
with a brighter LCD or an OLED back there,

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:15.660
maybe it could solve or
help some of these issues,

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:18.750
but that wouldn't fix the
other one, which is sharpness.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:21.300
So this is a 1080p display, as I've said,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.340
but it doesn't even really look like that.

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.540
I mean, I think if I asked you

00:05:24.540 --> 00:05:26.160
what resolution you think this is,

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:27.450
or if I did a blind test or something,

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:30.510
it would compare pretty
closely to a 720p display.

00:05:30.510 --> 00:05:33.330
I mean, text is soft,
edges of things are soft,

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.350
all the corners are soft.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.510
It just all kinda has a little
bit of a glow to the edges,

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:39.030
which, again, makes things
nice and easy to look at,

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.920
like nothing is harsh, or super bright,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:41.970
or fatiguing on the eyes,

00:05:41.970 --> 00:05:44.790
but if it's a sharp photo or
a video detail you're after,

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:45.960
then putting a bunch of layers

00:05:45.960 --> 00:05:47.850
between your eyeballs
and the actual display

00:05:47.850 --> 00:05:49.920
is not the best way to see those details.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:51.030
And then same thing with contrast.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.130
Just because of how much
brightness is limited,

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:55.410
the lack of contrast
becomes really obvious.

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:58.350
This one's rated at just
1500:1 contrast ratio,

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.810
which, even when compared
to other budget phones,

00:06:00.810 --> 00:06:01.643
is not that great.

00:06:01.643 --> 00:06:02.760
Oh, and then screen protectors.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:04.500
People usually put screen
protectors on their phones,

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.140
and this one would
obviously not make sense

00:06:07.140 --> 00:06:08.490
to put a screen protector on (chuckling).

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:11.190
So suddenly, in this world
of feature-rich smartphones

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.320
with all these big,
bright, clear displays,

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.880
this goes from being an advantage

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.160
to being a niche feature (chuckles),

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:19.290
because most people aren't willing

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:20.970
to sacrifice the downsides

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.400
that come with maybe a
little bit less eye strain

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:24.570
and less reflections

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:25.410
for a matte display.

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.930
This isn't like a desktop screen,

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.400
where most people are buying it

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:32.910
just based on the panel and
the visual display properties.

00:06:32.910 --> 00:06:35.940
There's a bunch of stuff about
a phone people care about,

00:06:35.940 --> 00:06:39.030
and it turns out people do
not like that trade-off.

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.070
So I've talked about all
kinds of other niche features

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:41.970
in the smartphone world,

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.320
and how they're awesome

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.140
but won't actually sell a ton of phones.

00:06:46.140 --> 00:06:48.360
This is, like, the perfect
addition to that pile.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:51.750
I mean, TCL has these
special NXTPAPER modes

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:52.890
in the software,

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:54.150
and when you turn one of them on,

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:55.830
one's called color paper,

00:06:55.830 --> 00:06:57.120
and it goes the extra mile

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:59.220
to, you know, turn down
saturation a little bit

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:00.990
and reduce the extra-vibrant colors.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:02.220
it adds some texture,

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:03.150
and everything,

00:07:03.150 --> 00:07:05.670
it literally looks like
your smartphone screen

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:06.990
is, like, a colored piece of paper,

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.480
with, like, your icons in
colored ink, moving around.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:12.030
And then you can go all
the way up to ink paper,

00:07:12.030 --> 00:07:13.320
which goes full black and white.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:14.790
It seems silly to most people,

00:07:14.790 --> 00:07:18.150
but when you compare it to
an actual E Ink display,

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:20.070
that's when it looks amazing.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:21.390
Like, an actual e-reader

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:23.760
is the least fatiguing
version on your eyes,

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:24.810
but if you've ever used one,

00:07:24.810 --> 00:07:27.180
you know that frame rates are pretty slow,

00:07:27.180 --> 00:07:28.560
Like, you can hardly get

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:30.930
like, even a real
page-turning book animation

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:33.930
on some of the absolute
bleeding-edge best versions we have,

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:36.060
so a smartphone with a
special hardware layer

00:07:36.060 --> 00:07:37.950
that gets close to the same visual level

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:41.790
but is 90 hertz, fully
responsive, full color,

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.620
and can do anything your phone can do

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:45.000
on top of being an e-reader,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:46.500
is technically awesome,

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:48.840
which is why TCL also makes
a tablet version of this

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.070
and another phone version of this.

00:07:50.070 --> 00:07:53.280
It is super cool, but almost
no one's gonna buy it,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.470
and that's why not every other phone

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.270
is going out to do this too,

00:07:57.270 --> 00:07:59.040
at least for now, anyway.

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.300
Thanks for watching.

00:08:00.300 --> 00:08:01.860
Catch you guys the next one.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:02.693
Peace.

